00:35:23.872 --> 00:35:25.080
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:25.917
And welcome, Bill.

00:35:25.917 --> 00:35:27.000
BILL DOUGHERTY: Thank you.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:28.583
THORSTEN LUEDECKE:
I would be curious,

00:35:28.583 --> 00:35:31.380
and probably the audience
is as curious as I am,

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.510
why did you choose
to actually start

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:36.360
researching email encryption
providers for Omada Health,

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:38.666
and what use cases does
Virtru solve for you?

00:35:38.666 --> 00:35:39.540
BILL DOUGHERTY: Sure.

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:43.140
So as a health care provider,
we're a regulated company,

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:46.450
and email is the bane of the
existence for most health care

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:48.330
providers, because
it's the easiest

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.580
way for protected
health information, PHI,

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:52.560
to leak out of your company.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:55.290
We had a solution that
was a gateway product.

00:35:55.290 --> 00:35:59.190
Meaning the e-mails were leaving
the company, would get scanned,

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.620
and if that solution thought
that it contained something

00:36:01.620 --> 00:36:03.510
sensitive, it would encrypt it.

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:06.150
The trouble is that, that
operator's a black box.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:08.190
Meaning we couldn't
customize the rules,

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:10.770
and the end user didn't
actually know if the email had

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:13.170
gone out encrypted or not.

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:15.360
The other big problem
we were trying to solve

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:16.920
is because it was
a gateway solution,

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.320
the email still stayed
unencrypted in the user's

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:20.670
sent mailbox.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:22.410
And so we looked
at that and said,

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:24.360
it wasn't secure
enough as a method

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:28.282
for protecting our participants'
very private information.

00:36:28.282 --> 00:36:29.490
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:31.323
Charles, maybe a follow
up question for you.

00:36:34.590 --> 00:36:36.280
With regards to
your customers, I

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:39.140
know you have so many customers,
and lots of our largest G Suite

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:41.550
customers are also
your customers.

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:44.150
Why are they adding Virtru
to their G Suite domain?

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:46.400
CHARLES GOLD: Yes,
so privacy has

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:50.370
become an incredibly hot topic
in virtually every industry.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:52.520
So we really see in our
thousands of customers

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:55.250
two primary drivers
at the highest level.

00:36:55.250 --> 00:36:58.430
So it's either
regulation, so HIPAA,

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:01.830
or CJIS, or FERPA, or any
of these regulatory regimes,

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:05.130
or data residency that
you see around the world.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:07.670
So that's probably
half of our customers

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:09.030
are choosing it for that reason.

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:11.310
The other half is what
I'd call business privacy.

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:13.970
So we've got media
companies who are choosing

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:16.670
us to protect source
information, entertainment

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:19.730
companies using us to protect
scripts, manufacturing

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:22.520
organizations using us
to protect manufacturing

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:24.450
plans and their supply chain.

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:27.860
So it's either business
privacy or regulation.

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:29.300
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:32.870
Bill, I think you alluded to
it to a certain extent before,

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:35.180
but I think what
would be interesting

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:38.250
is, what were the major benefits
that you've received out

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:41.756
of switching to Virtru from
the gateway solution before?

00:37:41.756 --> 00:37:43.130
BILL DOUGHERTY:
Yeah, so Virtru's

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:45.860
a very cool product in that
it runs on the client side,

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:49.330
it runs on a browser hook, and
that offered certain benefits

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:49.830
to us.

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:53.360
First of all, it gave the user
control over whether or not

00:37:53.360 --> 00:37:56.067
to encrypt a message or not.

00:37:56.067 --> 00:37:57.650
And when they do
choose to encrypt it,

00:37:57.650 --> 00:37:59.540
it gets persisted on
their side, and the

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:03.140
sent mail also encrypted.

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:05.970
But it's also a very
good educational tool.

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:09.230
In that you're able
to write rules for it

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:12.410
to flag what may be
sensitive information,

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:14.480
and if somebody hits
Send and forgets

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.950
to turn Virtru on, the browser
plugin will come up and say,

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.190
hey, I think your message
may be sensitive because

00:38:22.190 --> 00:38:23.510
of these parameters.

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:25.940
And the default behavior
then is a button that says,

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:26.900
Send Secure.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:31.070
So not only does it increase
the security overall of our mail

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:34.400
platform, but it helps
educate our end users

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:37.310
as to what actually constitutes
sensitive or private

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:38.900
information.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.340
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: And just
as a follow up for you,

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:44.060
so what are the features that
are most valuable from Virtru

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:45.312
for you?

00:38:45.312 --> 00:38:47.270
BILL DOUGHERTY: Number
one is the rules engine,

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:50.780
being able to craft rules
specific to our data.

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:54.350
We know that our
reports are probably

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:58.040
80% of the possibility
for information

00:38:58.040 --> 00:39:00.200
to leak out in an
unsecure manner.

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:01.980
And they follow a set pattern.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:04.820
So I was able to tailor a rule
specific to what our data looks

00:39:04.820 --> 00:39:08.210
like, and anytime a
report gets e-mailed,

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:10.250
if the person generating
it and sending it

00:39:10.250 --> 00:39:11.930
didn't already
flag it, it's going

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:14.240
to get flagged and sent secure.

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:18.470
The other is the browser plugin
gives the end user the ability

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:21.170
to set things like
additional controls

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:24.464
like disable forwarding,
expiration dates.

00:39:24.464 --> 00:39:26.630
And if they make a mistake,
they send it to somebody

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:28.580
and they didn't intend
to, they can instantly

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:30.110
revoke that message themselves.

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:32.152
They don't have to get an
administrator involved.

00:39:32.152 --> 00:39:33.359
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:39:33.359 --> 00:39:34.280
Thank you, Bill.

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:35.660
Charles, last question for you.

00:39:35.660 --> 00:39:38.630
Because I see people from Virtru
mostly on the engineering prog

00:39:38.630 --> 00:39:40.820
management side in our
building walking in and out

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:42.051
all the time.

00:39:42.051 --> 00:39:44.300
Can you share some of the
things that you're currently

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:46.720
working on that we
might see from Virtru?

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:48.479
We've got so
integrations very soon.

00:39:48.479 --> 00:39:49.770
CHARLES GOLD: Yeah, absolutely.

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:53.680
So one thing which is relatively
new, but is in the market today

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:55.400
is our customer key server.

00:39:55.400 --> 00:39:57.410
So we see many
customers with a desire

00:39:57.410 --> 00:39:59.600
to host their own
encryption keys

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:03.110
on their premises in a private
cloud, in a public cloud.

00:40:03.110 --> 00:40:06.890
That really gives them exclusive
control of their content.

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:09.770
So that's one big thing
which is in market today.

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:12.440
Things that we're working
on include our integration

00:40:12.440 --> 00:40:15.200
with Drive, which
is in beta, that

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:18.830
allows you to protect files
that you store in Drive,

00:40:18.830 --> 00:40:21.590
and have those protections
persist regardless of where

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:23.660
those files are shared.

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:27.446
And then secondly, we're working
on encrypted storage under GCP.

00:40:27.446 --> 00:40:28.820
So for organizations
that finally

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:31.500
want to migrate all of
those files and data

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:33.770
that they've been storing
on premise to the cloud,

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:36.135
but for regulatory or
business privacy reasons need

00:40:36.135 --> 00:40:38.622
to encrypt it, we will soon
have a solution in that space.

00:40:38.622 --> 00:40:39.830
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:41.900
Thank you, Charles,
and I thank all of you

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:43.850
for being on the second
group of the panel.

00:40:43.850 --> 00:40:45.620
And we're rotating
again, and I would

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.110
like to invite AODocs
and RingCentral on stage.

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:09.770
So next to me we
have Stephan Donze.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:12.860
Stephan is the founder
and CEO of AODocs,

00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:15.050
and AODocs is one
of our recommended

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:18.949
ISVs in the document management
system space, as well.

00:41:18.949 --> 00:41:20.240
Good to have you here, Stephan.

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:22.130
Who did you bring us today?

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:24.880
STEPHANE DONZE: So with
me is Rosemary Arce.

00:41:24.880 --> 00:41:31.190
She's the lead IT collaboration
architect at Seagate.

00:41:31.190 --> 00:41:33.010
So a few words about AODocs.

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:35.240
So AODocs has been
traded in 2012.

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:37.550
We're a document
management platform

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:38.570
built on top of Google.

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:40.880
Actually, we started
even before Google Drive

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:42.520
was officially released.

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:44.860
And with the vision
that we could combine

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:48.740
the ease of use of Google
and this capability in all

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:51.050
of the Googleness
of Google Drive

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:53.990
with the requirements
of enterprises

00:41:53.990 --> 00:41:56.700
about control and processes
in document management.

00:41:56.700 --> 00:42:01.130
So I think today AODocs is
kind of combining those two

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:05.870
worlds together to let
the enterprises apply

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:08.470
their compliance, their business
processes, their document

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:11.820
control, while enjoying
all of the ease of use

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:13.395
and the flexibility
of Google Drive.

00:42:13.395 --> 00:42:15.770
And I think Rosemary would
give you a few examples on how

00:42:15.770 --> 00:42:17.012
they do that at Seagate.

00:42:17.012 --> 00:42:18.220
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:20.480
Welcome Rosemary, and
good to see you again.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:22.910
Full disclosure for the
audience, Rosemary and I

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:26.150
have been in those type of
panel situations before.

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:28.100
And I can highly
recommend that whenever

00:42:28.100 --> 00:42:30.530
you have a question with
regards to AODocs and G Suite,

00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:32.420
please feel free to
reach out to Rosemary,

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:34.860
because she's extremely
knowledgeable.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:37.380
ROSEMARY ARCE: You have a hard
time stopping me sometimes.

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:38.710
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: I know.

00:42:38.710 --> 00:42:41.270
We make it work today.

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:43.614
So I think everybody's
curious, what

00:42:43.614 --> 00:42:44.780
was the baseline at Seagate?

00:42:44.780 --> 00:42:46.988
Seagate everybody knows, I
believe, in this audience,

00:42:46.988 --> 00:42:48.260
because it's a tech audience.

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:49.718
But what was the
baseline that made

00:42:49.718 --> 00:42:52.640
you consider a new document
management system solution?

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:55.635
ROSEMARY ARCE: At the time,
we had been a Google Apps

00:42:55.635 --> 00:42:56.135
customer.

00:42:56.135 --> 00:42:59.810
We're an early adopter, so
we'd been on it for five years.

00:42:59.810 --> 00:43:02.240
And we had made the
commitment to try to be

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:03.780
Cloud as much as we could.

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:07.490
But really, the impetus for
this was kind of interesting

00:43:07.490 --> 00:43:11.030
now thinking about it, given
all the ways I use AODocs now.

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:16.280
Was that I had an aging
Lotus Notes repository

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:18.140
called Lotus Notes
Quicker, and you

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:21.710
have to really go back in time
to figure out what that was.

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:24.830
From your perspective,
it was thousands

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:29.270
of databases in a Lotus
Notes proprietary format

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:31.220
that was UI based.

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:35.360
It had about four
terabytes of data.

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:38.180
Of the thousands and thousands
of documents in there,

00:43:38.180 --> 00:43:42.940
there were up to 200, to
300 versions per document,

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:45.830
And we wanted to move those.

00:43:45.830 --> 00:43:48.052
We needed to get out,
because basically IBM was

00:43:48.052 --> 00:43:49.010
going to pull the plug.

00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:50.990
We kept it as long as we could.

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:54.230
And we needed to migrate
that with all of the features

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:54.770
intact.

00:43:54.770 --> 00:43:57.290
So we weren't asking for much.

00:43:57.290 --> 00:43:59.060
I wanted a migration.

00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:02.300
I wanted all of the documents.

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:06.350
I wanted all of the versions,
along with the metadata

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:08.870
associated with each version.

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:11.270
And I wanted them
to be completely

00:44:11.270 --> 00:44:13.280
in the same hierarchy
and permissions

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:16.460
as they were when they were
in the other interface.

00:44:16.460 --> 00:44:17.672
And that's all I needed.

00:44:17.672 --> 00:44:19.380
THORSTEN LUEDECKE:
That's all you needed.

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:20.838
ROSEMARY ARCE:
That's all I needed.

00:44:20.838 --> 00:44:22.239
We're very pushy where we're at.

00:44:22.239 --> 00:44:24.530
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: And Stephan
and team made it happen,

00:44:24.530 --> 00:44:26.030
or what were the
key features that--

00:44:26.030 --> 00:44:29.180
ROSEMARY ARCE: So initially
we went out looking to see,

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:30.320
does anybody do that?

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:33.740
Does anyone migrate
what was at that point

00:44:33.740 --> 00:44:39.110
an 8-year-old product, and
take it out of Lotus Notes,

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:40.640
and move it anywhere?

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.080
I didn't even care where it was.

00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:45.140
USB stick, it would
have been fine.

00:44:45.140 --> 00:44:49.520
And it became quickly apparent
that that wasn't an easy thing

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:50.760
to find.

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:56.210
And we discovered that AODocs
had a migration tool for Lotus

00:44:56.210 --> 00:45:00.800
Notes, not specifically for
Quicker, so we talked to them.

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:03.230
And then we were pleasantly
surprised that not only

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:06.440
did they have a migration
tool, but their end state

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:09.410
for migration, Google
Drive was an option,

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:12.230
and using the Google
infrastructure was an option.

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:14.180
And they all supported
some of the features

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:16.140
that I didn't mention
which oh, by the way,

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:20.000
we need workflow
associated with this,

00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:24.380
and we need to be able
to get all the components

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:26.780
and put Humpty Dumpty
back together again.

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.000
And we need check in,
check out capability

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:31.100
of all of these documents.

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:33.320
Which is why we had kind of
written off Google Drive,

00:45:33.320 --> 00:45:36.570
because we knew it couldn't
do any of the above.

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:40.299
And so then we started
the inquisition of AODocs.

00:45:40.299 --> 00:45:41.840
THORSTEN LUEDECKE:
And then could you

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:44.540
speak to what was the
impact at Seagate?

00:45:44.540 --> 00:45:48.530
ROSEMARY ARCE: So we went with
the migration tool, the impact

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:50.000
first of all, was huge.

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:52.520
It did what we asked it to.

00:45:52.520 --> 00:45:55.692
If you were to look at a
10-year-old Lotus Quicker--

00:45:55.692 --> 00:45:57.650
and we had do it in place,
because some of them

00:45:57.650 --> 00:46:01.050
were legally bound
to not lose the data.

00:46:01.050 --> 00:46:03.410
If you were to look
at a single document,

00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:05.750
look at the permissions and
see where it was located,

00:46:05.750 --> 00:46:08.300
and who it had access to, you
would find the exact same thing

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:09.740
in AODocs.

00:46:09.740 --> 00:46:13.490
You would find if you wanted
to know what Version 201 looked

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:15.710
like, and who
edited it, and what

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:20.560
data edited it, to this day you
would still see exactly that.

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:24.490
They provided the web UI that
the group was familiar with.

00:46:24.490 --> 00:46:27.820
We actually didn't want them
to look like Google Drive,

00:46:27.820 --> 00:46:30.430
even though they could
have with the product.

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:32.110
We said, no, we want the web UI.

00:46:32.110 --> 00:46:34.150
It looks virtually identical.

00:46:34.150 --> 00:46:37.600
We were able to create
templates that looked identical.

00:46:37.600 --> 00:46:41.230
And for those databases
that had workflow,

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:42.490
the workflow is there.

00:46:42.490 --> 00:46:45.730
It does, and in fact, can
do more than it could ever

00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:47.980
do in Lotus Notes.

00:46:47.980 --> 00:46:53.200
So that combined
with the metadata.

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:56.920
And you know, I want to
underscore the metadata.

00:46:56.920 --> 00:47:00.550
In the Lotus Notes environment,
when you have a record,

00:47:00.550 --> 00:47:03.970
there can be six files
associated with that record,

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:07.030
and there can be different
properties associated

00:47:07.030 --> 00:47:08.470
with the file.

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:11.770
So it wasn't a Google
Drive equals one file,

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:14.920
and AODocs equals one file.

00:47:14.920 --> 00:47:18.820
I wanted him to put
the record up there,

00:47:18.820 --> 00:47:22.300
point it to the exact six
files that were associated,

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:26.830
and all of the fields that
were associated with that file.

00:47:26.830 --> 00:47:34.110
And they did that
with very little--

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:35.980
I scheduled all kinds
of training sessions.

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:37.800
We're talking about 1500 users.

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:40.200
I was ready, I was going
to have to train them.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:42.360
I ended up canceling
most of them.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:45.780
I only had to do three
or four, I recorded them,

00:47:45.780 --> 00:47:47.940
and people did
not ask questions,

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.400
they just kept working.

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:52.560
THORSTEN LUEDECKE: Excellent.

00:47:52.560 --> 00:47:53.890
Very good, very good.

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:55.740
And I know how pleased
